welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Maurice Samuels the Betty Jane Ann  lien professor of French at Yale  University he specializes in the  literature and culture of 19th century  France and in Jewish Studies his first  book the spectacular past popular  history and the novel in 19th century  France when the Gattis Smith  International Book Prize awarded by the  Macmillan Center today we'll talk with  Professor Samuels about his second book  inventing the Israelite Jewish fiction  in 19th century France which brings to  light an almost totally unknown area of  fiction in France  welcome professor Samuels thank you  let's begin with the book give us an  overview of it well the book is about  the first Jewish fiction in French and  by Jewish fiction I mean fiction by Jews  about Jews and largely for Jews produced  in France starting at around 1830 so  France was you know the first country  that emancipated the Jews that gave Jews  full civil rights and it happened in  France much earlier than in other  countries during the French Revolution  in 1790 and 91 so my theory here is that  fiction offered the first generation of  Jews who had been born French citizens  and who came of age around 1830 that had  offered them a kind of laboratory for  experimenting with the perils but also  the possibilities of their new  historical situation so you know what  did it mean that they were French  citizens did it mean that they had to  give up their Jewish identity totally  did it mean that they had to change it  in some way or did it mean that in order  to stay Jewish they would have to  renounce their French identity so my  theory is that they tried to come to  terms with those dilemmas by inventing  stories about them okay and they're  writing in French they're writing in  French yes it's all in French yeah so  what kind of stories were they writing  what were they writing about well there  were lots of different kinds of stories  and  one big sort of theme was romantic  dilemmas so often you get these stories  and these are very typical of the time  in a way there were lots of kind of  sentimental novels about women who were  falling in love with someone like a kind  of Romeo and Juliet story someone who  their family didn't like these Jewish  writers transposed that story to come to  terms with these Jewish dilemmas so you  would often have a young beautiful  Jewish woman who falls in love with a  handsome Christian nobleman and it  causes her family lots of stress and I  try to argue that this isn't just a kind  of typical romance plot but or you know  well it's not a typical romance plot but  it's also trying to kind of come to  terms with these central problems like  intermarriage so you know the weight of  the past of tradition you know should  they be loyal to Judaism or should they  can they kind of declare their autonomy  or did you find any kind of you know any  kind of controversial kind of writings  yeah well a lot of these writings were  controversial for the Jewish communities  well they were a lot of them tried to  come to terms with really kind of  burning questions at the time everything  from should we maintain all the Sabbath  rules you know in her marriage as I said  was a burning question then as it is now  you know what happens yeah what did what  did happen during that time period were  there intra marriages wow that's a good  question actually historians other than  myself have determined that actually  France had relatively low levels of  intermarriage and conversion compared to  Germany and to other countries partly  because Fran French Jews had much less  to gain from it in a sense because they  were full equal citizens they didn't  have to try to be sure so this is kind  of you know cutting-edge trailblazing in  a way because this is the first time  anyone has really looked at these kinds  of books what led you to pursue this  well part  the historical question so historians  have often given 19th century French  Jews a bad rap they've accused them of  being total assimilators this was the  kind of traditional view that they 19th  century French Jews as soon as they  could become French citizens gladly  abandoned their Jewish identity and  wanted nothing more to do with it and  this was all fine  until the Dreyfus Affair at the end of  the 19th century and then of course  World War two when things went very bad  so I and and this was also we see this  actually in how they've looked at Jewish  literature so I teach a course on Jews  in modern France and I still use this  same textbook that has a chapter on  Jewish literature that says there was no  Jewish literature in France before the  20th century now I knew from my first  book which was on historical  representation in France that this  wasn't totally true so when I was  researching that book I came across a  historical novel called leisure Eve the  Jew s written by this woman named  Eugenie foois from the 1830s and I start  was a little curious about it so I you  know I read it and it was all about a  kind of Jewish woman having this  romantic dilemma was the one who wrote  the book well that's what I didn't know  so I did a little bit of research and I  found out that she was in fact from a  very prominent Jewish family from  Bordeaux and she wrote a bunch of novels  at the time about Jews so when I came  across this chapter saying that there  was no Jewish fiction in French before  1900 I knew it wasn't true and that's  the kind of when that happens it's a  scholar you know you have to keep  researching it so you know when I  decided to write this book I went back  to Eugenie foois tried to figure out did  she write other things were there others  like her so that's really how I came to  okay and how how did you do the research  I mean how many books did you end up  finding yeah well that's you know it was  hard because I knew the detective I  imagined yeah and a lot of time in the  library in Paris and  which is you know not such a terrible  place to have to do your research but so  I knew that she existed and I knew she  had written a bunch of other things but  how did I you know how do you find out  whether there were other people like her  so the first thing I did was a they  published lists of every novel published  in the 19th century and so the library  has those lists I started to go through  them to look for anything kind of Jewish  in the title like a Jewish name or you  know the title like the Jew s yes how  many books were on that list there were  thousands of books yes oh yeah people  were very privileged yes well you know  that the novel was everything in well  theatre too but you know there were a  lot of novels being published so you  know I found some stuff but I had no  idea whether you know and it was really  too hard I couldn't really tell if I was  missing things so the big break came  when um I started to look at the Jewish  newspapers now I knew that they had  published these Jewish newspapers  starting in 1840 there was a kind of  reformed one and then there was an  Orthodox rival the reformed one is from  1840 the Orthodox one 1844 so I knew  about them I kind of looked at them but  I started to look at them really  carefully and I realized that they  published fictions so there was all this  serial fiction all of it met my  definition by Jews about Jews and for  Jews and and even more importantly they  reviewed every novel or even play that  was about Jews and so that really  convinced me that there was something  like a Jewish literary sphere in the mid  19th century that this was much more  than the case of one single author in  fact there was a lot going on how  amazing is that that I read in the  textbook there's this doesn't exist and  then to say wow there's there's and how  many books do you think well I that I  find it's in my book on there were um  you know dozens and dozens of Jewish  writers not all of them wrote about Jews  so I only talked about the ones that  really directly thematized questions of  Jewish identity at the time and so I  have six chapters in the book and I  would say I talked about maybe  eight to ten writers in detail I  mentioned a lot of other ones and each  one of those what I tried to focus on  ones each one represents a different  kind of point on the ideological  spectrum from total assimilation some of  them did want to totally assimilate to  reform like will change the religion to  a kind of version of neo-orthodoxy of  kind of returning to the kind of  tradition and what I'm trying to argue  is that the picture that we get of Jews  is much more complicated than this idea  that they were total assimilators in  fact there were lots of responses so I  was deranged on the spectrum yeah yeah  so how many were was it mostly man  riding or was it women or was it pretty  equal yeah so the first one is I really  determined that usually nifo I had been  the first one you know and in a way so a  woman in a way that's not totally  surprising because at the beginning of  in the 18th century in the beginning of  the 19th century the novel was still  kind of written a lot of times by women  and for a female audience so it's not  surprising that a woman would be the  first one but she's really my kind of my  only woman in fact most of the rest of  them were men interesting and how you  know how astute was the writing  I mean quality wise I'm curious yeah  that's a question that I get a lot are  these writers any good and you know did  I find a Kafka you know a kind of  diamond in the rough that we didn't know  about the answer is pretty much no I  didn't find any you know quote unquote  great writer  in fact these writers are mostly working  according to kind of the generic  conventions of the day so they're  writing kind of you know very typical  novels but um one thing that really  surprised me was that the more I started  to read them the more I started to  appreciate them and to like them and to  even think that they were actually  pretty good so it was a matter of  recovering the kind of questions that  they were answering and once I knew what  those were  I start to realize how actually  inventive they were both you know  ideologically but also literally and I  started to kind of appreciate the way  the small and kind of subtle ways that  they deviated from those generic  conventions started to seem very clever  to me so I don't know if I have been  kind of you know I just read too many of  these novels but I did start to  appreciate them I'm just saying so now  what do you think is gonna happen do you  think you know more and more you know  more and more people will be now aware  that these things exist  my hope is that I can try to you know  get these novels into circulation so you  know are these gonna be put on every PhD  reading list  probably not are they you know but I am  I already edited last year a an  anthology in English of some of the of  my favorite ones where I did  translations of them and I'm next year  that might this book is being translated  into French and to go along with the  publication of it I'm also editing an  anthology in French of of these stories  so I'm hoping that they'll have a life  and there's there's there's starting to  be some signs I was actually asked to be  on a PhD thesis defense in at NYU of  someone who had worked on this after  reading my book so I was kind of excited  about that and I'm feeding them to my  students here too so exactly it's  exciting that these things no one had  basically had them for 150 years and now  well thank you so much for being here  today and sharing some of your work well  thank you it was a pleasure okay for  more information about Professor Samuels  and his research please visit our  website at galley D you slash Macmillan  report be sure to join us again for  another episode of the Macmillan report  made possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale

